<h2>5. Conclusion</h2>
<p>Search is still most of Alphabet, but less of it each quarter. It supplied 52.8% of revenue and 67.0% of Services-plus-Cloud operating profit in Q2 2026, and both shares have fallen steadily since Q1 2024, with profit share falling fastest because Cloud carries no TAC and its margin has nearly quadrupled. What is being cannibalized is the traditional results page. Search grew 16.8% in Q2, but the growth came from paid clicks that arrived with ads in AI Overviews and AI Mode, and our estimates put the page of links at roughly 2% growth once those surfaces are stripped out. Network, the one filed line with no AI offset, is already shrinking, with impressions down 12% and revenue down six quarters running. What is replacing that page is Cloud, which now earns 22% of Alphabet&#39;s operating income, converts 54 cents of each new revenue dollar to profit, holds a $514B backlog that management says it is still supply-constrained to serve, and has added TPU system sales as a second revenue line. Our estimates carry wide error bars, but every measure points the same way, and we expect Cloud&#39;s share of operating profit to reach about half by 2028. The equity is a bet that Cloud scales fast enough to replace the growth Search gives up, and the market prices that bet at about one chance in four of reaching Cantor&#39;s $420 target, which is 28x consensus 2027 EPS, and 45% odds of finishing below today&#39;s price.</p>
<p><strong>The next test is the Q3 print on October 28.</strong></p>

